WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.040
In the early Western Jin, the high official Shi Chong was extremely rich and always loved showing off his wealth.

00:00:08.390 --> 00:00:12.950
His home was filled everywhere with gold, silver and jewels, shining dazzlingly.

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:19.900
The emperor's uncle Wang Kai refused to be outdone, and the two began a contest over who could be more wasteful.

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:24.642
Neither would give in to the other, and the contest grew livelier and livelier.

00:00:24.992 --> 00:00:31.160
Wang Kai actually used sugar water to wash his pots — already unbelievably extravagant for the time.

00:00:31.510 --> 00:00:37.462
Unexpectedly, Shi Chong went even further, actually using candles as firewood to cook rice.

00:00:37.812 --> 00:00:42.204
When the candles burned down he simply replaced them with new ones, without a care.

00:00:42.554 --> 00:00:50.258
Even the coral tree the Emperor Wu had given Wang Kai, Shi Chong smashed — and then brought out still more, and bigger, ones.

00:00:50.608 --> 00:00:54.952
Wang Kai was so angry he could not speak, and even the emperor could do nothing.

00:00:55.302 --> 00:01:01.734
From then on, 'Shi Chong competes in wealth' became an expression used specifically for extravagant, wasteful behavior.

